       {
        "id": 3537521,
        "postDate": "2026-10-12T18:08:25.220Z",
        "rawMarkdown": "Thanks a lot, that’s fair, and I get why you can’t go too specific. A few narrow questions, answer only what you’re comfortable with:\n\nIs the label gain mostly from how you read the reports (prompt/LLM, following the host’s image-based definitions), or from re-labelling with your own image models (pseudo-labels / OOF predictions)?\nFor findings the report doesn’t mention at all (e.g. synovitis), do you treat them as negative, infer them from related findings (e.g. effusion), or let a model fill them in?\nDo you keep labels soft (e.g. 0.7 for “small” or “possible”) or hard 0/1?\nDid you use the 58 gold studies to tune your label mapping, or keep them only for validation?\nRoughly how big was the label effect on your LB, closer to +0.005 or +0.02?\n\nEven a one-word answer to any of these would help a lot. Thanks, and good luck in the final stretch",
        "content": "<p>Thanks a lot, that’s fair, and I get why you can’t go too specific. A few narrow questions, answer only what you’re comfortable with:</p>\n<p>Is the label gain mostly from how you read the reports (prompt/LLM, following the host’s image-based definitions), or from re-labelling with your own image models (pseudo-labels / OOF predictions)?\nFor findings the report doesn’t mention at all (e.g. synovitis), do you treat them as negative, infer them from related findings (e.g. effusion), or let a model fill them in?\nDo you keep labels soft (e.g. 0.7 for “small” or “possible”) or hard 0/1?\nDid you use the 58 gold studies to tune your label mapping, or keep them only for validation?\nRoughly how big was the label effect on your LB, closer to +0.005 or +0.02?</p>\n<p>Even a one-word answer to any of these would help a lot. Thanks, and good luck in the final stretch</p>",
        "parent": {},
